      title: "Hammurabi of Babylon",
      body: `## A small kingdom

**Hammurabi** came to the throne of **Babylon** around **1792 BC**, the sixth king of an Amorite dynasty that had ruled the city for about a century. Babylon was then a modest kingdom, one of several in southern Mesopotamia, surrounded by more powerful neighbors: Larsa to the south, ruled by the long-lived **Rim-Sin**; Eshnunna to the east; Shamshi-Adad's kingdom to the north; and Elam beyond the mountains. For the first thirty years of his reign, Hammurabi was a careful, patient ruler, who built canals and temples, strengthened his city's walls, and managed a complex web of alliances, as the Mari letters show.

## Conquest

In the last decade of his reign, Hammurabi transformed Babylon into the dominant power of Mesopotamia. After an alliance with Mari and others defeated an invasion by the Elamites, he turned on his rivals one by one. In **1763 BC**, after a long siege, he conquered **Larsa** and captured Rim-Sin, who had ruled for some sixty years, uniting the south. He defeated Eshnunna. In **1761 BC**, he turned against his former ally, Zimri-Lim of **Mari**, conquered the city, and later destroyed its walls and palace, burying the archive that Parrot would find nearly four thousand years later. By the end of his reign, around **1750 BC**, Hammurabi ruled all of southern Mesopotamia and much of the north.

## The administrator

Hammurabi's letters, especially those to his officials in the conquered south, show a king who involved himself in the smallest details of government. He ordered canals to be dredged, settled disputes over land and debts, ordered the punishment of corrupt officials, arranged the delivery of sheep and barley, adjusted the calendar by adding an extra month, and dealt with complaints from individual subjects. In one letter, he orders an official to investigate a man who has taken a bribe; in another, he orders that a field wrongly seized be returned to its owner. The letters show a ruler who presented himself as personally responsible for justice throughout his kingdom.

## Marduk and Babylon

Hammurabi's rise brought new prestige to Babylon and its god, **Marduk**, who had been a minor deity. Over the following centuries, as Babylon became the religious and cultural center of Mesopotamia, Marduk rose to become the king of the gods, displacing Enlil. The Babylonian creation epic, which we will study later in this lesson, tells how Marduk won his supremacy. Babylon itself would remain the most important city of Mesopotamia, and one of the most famous cities in the world, for more than fifteen hundred years.

## The dynasty after Hammurabi

Hammurabi's empire did not long survive him. Under his son, **Samsu-iluna** (r. c. 1749–1712 BC), the south rebelled, and the old Sumerian cities, including Ur and Uruk, suffered destruction and were partly abandoned, perhaps because of the rebellions, perhaps because of changes in the courses of the rivers and the salinization of the soil. A new dynasty, called the **Sealand** dynasty, arose in the far south. Babylon's kingdom shrank to the area around the capital. The dynasty of Hammurabi lasted another century and a half, until Babylon was sacked by the Hittites in 1595 BC.

## Salt in the soil

The decline of the southern cities in this period has been linked to a long-term environmental problem: the **salinization** of the soil. Irrigation water carries small amounts of salt, which, in the hot, dry climate of southern Mesopotamia, accumulates in the soil as the water evaporates, unless the fields are left fallow and the salt is flushed out. In 1958, the scholars **Thorkild Jacobsen** and **Robert McC. Adams** argued that records of crop yields showed a shift from wheat to barley, which is more tolerant of salt, and a decline in yields over the third and early second millennia BC, as the soil became saltier. Their argument has been debated, but salinization remains a problem in Iraq today, and it is a reminder that the irrigation that made Mesopotamian civilization possible also slowly undermined it.

## The image of the king

Hammurabi presented himself to his people and to posterity as a king of justice, the "shepherd" of his people, chosen by the gods to bring order to the land. This image was expressed above all in his famous collection of laws, inscribed on stone monuments set up in the temples of his kingdom near the end of his reign. To that monument we now turn.

## The lost city

Strangely, we know very little about the city of Babylon in Hammurabi's time. The great city that the German archaeologist **Robert Koldewey** excavated between 1899 and 1917, with its famous Ishtar Gate and processional way, is the Babylon of Nebuchadnezzar II, more than a thousand years later. The remains of Hammurabi's Babylon lie beneath it, below the modern water table, which has risen over the centuries, so that they cannot be reached by ordinary excavation. Most of what we know about Hammurabi comes not from his capital but from the letters and documents found at other cities—Mari, Larsa, Sippar—and from his monuments.

Mesopotamian kings named each year of their reigns after an important event of the previous year—"the year Hammurabi dug the canal called 'Hammurabi is abundance for the people,'" or "the year he defeated the army of Elam." The list of Hammurabi's year names is one of our main sources for the sequence of events of his reign. For his first three decades, the year names record mostly building, canals, and temple offerings; in the last decade, they record conquest after conquest. The change is striking, and it tells its own story.

> **Pause and consider.** Hammurabi's letters show a king involved in the smallest details of government. Why might a ruler of a large kingdom want to be seen as personally responsible for justice?`,
